---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { DATA_VERSION, DATA_SOURCE } from '../../lib/vat';

const title = 'OSS VAT Explained: EU One-Stop Shop Guide | VatSnap';
const description =
  'What the EU VAT One-Stop Shop (OSS) is, who can use it, and how to find each buyer-country rate. For Etsy, Shopify and freelance sellers.';
const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'OSS VAT Explained: the EU One-Stop Shop for Small Sellers',
  description,
  datePublished: '2026-09-24',
  dateModified: DATA_VERSION,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'VatSnap', url: 'https://vatratefinder.com/' },
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://vatratefinder.com/blog/oss-vat-explained/',
};
const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://vatratefinder.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: 'https://vatratefinder.com/blog/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'OSS VAT Explained' },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/blog/oss-vat-explained/"
  schema={[articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <p class="meta"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/blog/">Guides</a> › OSS VAT Explained</p>
  </nav>
  <h1>OSS VAT Explained: the EU One-Stop Shop for Small Sellers</h1>
  <p class="meta">Last updated: {DATA_VERSION} · Source: {DATA_SOURCE} · 6 min read</p>

  <p class="lede">
    If you sell to consumers in other EU countries — on Etsy, Shopify, or as a
    freelancer selling downloads — the EU One-Stop Shop (OSS) lets you report
    that VAT through a single return in your home country instead of
    registering for VAT in every country you sell into.
  </p>

  <h2>What the OSS is</h2>
  <p>
    The One-Stop Shop is an EU-wide filing scheme for cross-border
    business-to-consumer (B2C) sales. You register once, charge the buyer's
    country VAT rate on each sale, and declare it all on one OSS return. It
    most commonly covers B2C goods and digital services — for example an
    Etsy printable sold from Germany to a buyer in
    <a href="/vat-rate/france/">France (20% standard rate)</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it suits</h2>
  <p>
    It suits small sellers with cross-border consumer sales who would
    otherwise face a registration in each buyer's country. Separate local
    registrations are still needed in some cases — for example if you hold
    stock in another country — so confirm your position with an accountant.
    Sales to VAT-registered businesses in another EU country are generally
    handled differently (often zero-rated with reverse charge).
  </p>

  <h2>Which rate to charge on each sale</h2>
  <p>
    Under OSS you still charge the buyer's country rate — the scheme changes
    where you report, not which rate applies. Look up the destination in the
    <a href="/vat-rate/">full EU VAT rates table (all 27 countries + UK)</a>:
    for instance <a href="/vat-rate/germany/">Germany charges 19%</a>,
    <a href="/vat-rate/ireland/">Ireland 23%</a>, and
    <a href="/vat-rate/hungary/">Hungary 27%</a>. Then compute the
    VAT-inclusive price with the <a href="/">free VAT calculator</a>, or check
    <a href="/blog/reverse-vat-calculation/">how to reverse-calculate VAT</a>
    when a supplier quotes you gross prices.
  </p>

  <h2>How to get started</h2>
  <p>
    Registration runs through your own country's tax portal, and returns are
    typically filed quarterly. Keep per-country sales records so each line of
    the return matches the rate you charged. Your accountant can confirm
    whether your products and customer types qualify before your first
    filing.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="btn" href="/">Open the VAT calculator</a>
  </p>
  <p class="meta">
    Guidance only — this guide explains general principles, not your tax
    position. Confirm with your accountant or the official EU source before
    registering or filing.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
